00:14:06,620 --> 00:14:09,500
about marriage and family being a mistake.

256
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:13,900
Right. Right? Yes.

257
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:16,060
Where did you get an idea like that?

258
00:14:16,060 --> 00:14:22,700
From you. Oh, honey, you know that I don't feel that way at all.

259
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:25,180
I mean, I love being married to your dad.

260
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:26,640
I love raising you kids.

261
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:27,540
Ask anyone we know.

262
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:28,180
Give me a phone.

263
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:31,280
I'll dial. Mom, why are you getting so excited?

264
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:34,640
Why? Because I get the feeling that we haven't been talking about you skipping a

265
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:36,980
grade. We've been talking about you skipping life.

266
00:14:37,260 --> 00:14:40,180
But for as long as I can remember, you've told me to work hard and think about

267
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:41,160
college and a career.

268
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:41,880
Sure, I did.

269
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,100
So what's the point of doing that?

270
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:46,040
Just to get married and to give it up like you did.

271
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:48,180
I haven't given up anything.

272
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:51,300
Mom, you work for Newsweek and quit to raise kids.

273
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:54,860
Yes, but now I've gone back to work and...

274
00:14:54,860 --> 00:14:56,220
For a local newspaper.

275
00:14:57,320 --> 00:15:01,300
The Long Island Herald is number one in Seyosset and number two in Massapequa.

276
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:08,480
Exactly. Oh, Carol, look, honey, I know this is a little complicated.

277
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:15,540
I mean, sure, I'm sorry that I missed the big time, but I'm not sorry at all about

278
00:15:15,540 --> 00:15:18,200
getting married or having three great kids.

279
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:20,940
Do you understand?

280
00:15:23,560 --> 00:15:26,180
Yes. Are you sure?

281
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:32,220
Of course, you think you have to say this because you regret what you had to do.

282
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:34,200
Oh no, no, no.

283
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:39,860
Of course you do, and don't worry about it because I am gonna make you so proud of

284
00:15:39,860 --> 00:15:41,560
me that you are gonna go crazy.

285
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:44,500
I'm already there.

286
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:49,160
So you see, Kitty, I used to be much older than this.

287
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:53,180
Oh, dear! It's an extremely rare disease.

288
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:55,260
That is really amazing.

289
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:58,040
Oh. Oh, and tragic, too.

290
00:15:58,140 --> 00:15:59,400
I would think so.

291
00:15:59,860 --> 00:16:03,640
Yeah, I mean, as far as I know, there's only two reported cases in the world.

292
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:05,560
Me and, uh, Dick Clark.

293
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:09,740
But you don't show your pain.

294
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:14,400
No. No, I don't.

295
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:17,940
I mean, do you have any idea what it's like going through puberty backwards?

296
00:16:19,220 --> 00:16:20,580
I can imagine.

297
00:16:22,580 --> 00:16:25,960
If they ever find a cure for this, give me a call.

298
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:28,680
You can count on it.

299
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:40,980
I have seen the future.

300
00:16:42,740 --> 00:16:44,600
And it is good.

301
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:49,080
Jason, do me a favor.

302
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:53,260
Shoot me. We'll be quick and clean.

303
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:55,240
We can do it on the front lawn, turn on the sprinklers.

304
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:56,260
No mess at all.

305
00:16:58,180 --> 00:16:59,260
So it went well.

306
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:02,140
Oh, honey, this is all my fault.

307
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:03,160
And do you know why?

308
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,000
Of course you know why.

309
00:17:05,040 --> 00:17:05,880
You know everything.

310
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:07,300
Well, it's my...

311
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:11,520
She thinks I'm some kind of a saint who gave up everything for you and the kids.

312
00:17:12,020 --> 00:17:13,700
Does she know that's the choice you made?

313
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:16,640
Yes. And she didn't believe me.

314
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:20,560
Oh, I wish she'd been around before she was born.

315
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:25,640
You know, that might work.

316
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:28,240
Don't humor me when I don't make any sense.

317
00:17:29,740 --> 00:17:32,920
Oh, honey, if you have any ideas, I'd love to hear them.

318
00:17:36,660 --> 00:17:38,200
I didn't think so.

319
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:00,940
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

320
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:02,140
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

321
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:05,200
I'm sorry. No, why do you say that?

322
00:18:05,420 --> 00:18:09,040
Because every time you get out the old tape recorder is to play some terrible old

323
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:11,520
song. Well, Gary Puckett happens to be an artist.

324
00:18:12,440 --> 00:18:14,020
And that's not what this is about.

325
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:16,820
I still have a lot of studying to do, Dad.

326
00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:19,560
Well, just listen when you have a chance.

327
00:18:20,760 --> 00:18:23,040
Listen. It's a blast from the past.

328
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:28,020
1971. It is Gary Puckett.

329
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,140
You're in the way, Mr.

330
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:44,420
Seaver. I'm a doctor.

331
00:18:44,980 --> 00:18:45,160
So am I.

332
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:46,020
You're still in the way.

333
00:18:46,260 --> 00:18:46,900
Come on, honey.

334
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:47,700
One more push.

335
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:49,260
Sure. Where else am I going to go?

336
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:53,700
Come on. One, two, three, and push!

337
00:18:53,820 --> 00:18:54,500
Here it comes.

338
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:55,820
I see him!

339
00:18:55,980 --> 00:18:57,120
A little more, Maggie.

340
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:02,200
Ah! Ah! Ah!

341
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:04,420
Wow, just look at him!

342
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,360
Her, her! It's a girl!

343
00:19:06,460 --> 00:19:07,260
It's a girl!

344
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:08,540
We have a daughter!

345
00:19:10,820 --> 00:19:12,840
Hello, Alexis. Not Alexis.

346
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:13,980
But we have granddaughters.

347
00:19:13,980 --> 00:19:15,680
That's what I thought it would be, a boy.

348
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:17,340
Nurse. Well, we...

349
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:19,520
Oh, isn't she beautiful, Jason?

350
00:19:19,580 --> 00:19:21,400
I don't care what we call her.

351
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:24,200
This is the happiest day of my life.

352
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:25,660
She looks like you.

353
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:27,280
Where is she going to go to college?

354
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:29,880
Before we decide on a college, shouldn't we give her a name?

355
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:31,920
Well, Jason, maybe Carol won't go to college.

356
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:34,540
Carol? I've always liked that name.

357
00:19:35,020 --> 00:19:37,340
Do you? Well, I...

358
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:42,760
Oh, I don't care what she wants to do just as long as she's as happy as I am right

359
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:44,780
now. Carol, it is then.

360
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:48,040
Oh, Jason, I love you.

361
00:19:48,960 --> 00:20:00,200
Ah! Ah! Does that sound like a woman who regretted her choice?

362
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:03,540
I always thought...

363
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:06,860
Oh, boy, oh, boy, oh, boy, were you wrong.

364
00:20:07,800 --> 00:20:11,040
So, you did what you wanted to do.

365
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:13,160
I still am?

366
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:16,620
So, you're saying I should have a family?

367
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:24,280
No. So, you're saying I should have a career?

368
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:30,100
No. Mom, I'm really getting confused here.

369
00:20:31,820 --> 00:20:36,140
Good, because that means you're beginning to realize that you have options.

370
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:42,660
Well, as long as we're set up, how about some classic Gary Puckett?

371
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:49,400
Hot. Was she hot?

372
00:20:51,040 --> 00:20:56,000
Psss. Well, let's just say that on a scale of one to ten, she was a 747.

373
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:03,040
Seven. So my mom says, take that lewd blouse back, young lady.

374
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:05,860
Well, your mother's great compared to my mother.

375
00:21:06,040 --> 00:21:07,760
I swear, I have, like, no choices.

376
00:21:08,240 --> 00:21:11,220
I mean, it's like she's sick of her life, so she wants to lead mine.

377
00:21:11,620 --> 00:21:13,060
My mother's not like that.

378
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:17,840
Are you talking to us?

379
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,380
Yeah, my mother lets me make my own choices.

380
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:25,000
She wants me to make my own choices.

381
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:30,460
She expects me to make my own choices.

382
00:21:31,860 --> 00:21:33,700
Isn't that incredible?

383
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:37,040
Too much studying.

384
00:21:37,620 --> 00:21:39,700
No, no. See?

385
00:21:39,900 --> 00:21:41,380
It's all up to me.

386
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:45,660
Uh -huh. Do I really want to spend every lunch hour studying?

387
00:21:46,060 --> 00:21:48,800
Do I really want all the pressure?

388
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:51,380
Do I really want to skip this grade?

389
00:21:54,240 --> 00:22:02,100
No. No. No, I do not want to skip this grade.

390
00:22:04,340 --> 00:22:07,140
All right, this is my day.

391
00:22:09,380 --> 00:22:14,520
Mike Siever. Mike Siever, I want you in my office in five minutes, and this time

392
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:15,780
there is no mistake.

393
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:16,780
Oh, my God.

394
00:22:24,460 --> 00:22:27,940
Mom, Mom, Carol displayed that tape you made when she was born.

395
00:22:28,260 --> 00:22:33,860
Oh, again. Yeah, I didn't even know tape recorders were invented in the olden days.

396
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:37,940
Oh, yes, we came up with that right after fire.

397
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:42,960
Well, when do I get to know the tape you made when I was born?

398
00:22:44,020 --> 00:22:45,580
Uh, your tape?

399
00:22:47,420 --> 00:22:52,660
Um... Well, you see, Ben, old buddy, when first you have children, you want to

400
00:22:52,660 --> 00:22:53,800
capture every moment.

401
00:22:53,860 --> 00:22:56,960
And then... I think we probably packed yours in a different box.

402
00:22:57,480 --> 00:22:58,240
I'll get it in the morning.

403
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:01,760
All right! Hmm.

404
00:23:01,820 --> 00:23:04,120
Jason, we didn't make a tape when Ben was born.

405
00:23:04,260 --> 00:23:04,880
Well, not yet.

406
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:06,060
We haven't. Oh, wait a minute.

407
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:08,640
Now, you remember how it goes.

408
00:23:08,880 --> 00:23:10,840
One, two, three, pull.

409
00:23:10,860 --> 00:23:11,240
Oh.
